  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nHigh pressure will build east across the region tonight, and \ntake residence across the Northeast CONUS through the weekend. \nThis pattern will result in dry conditions, light winds, mostly \nsunny/clear skies, cool nights, seasonably warm days and \ncomfortable humidity levels. Dewpoints are expected to remain in\nthe 40s(mountains) to 50s(lower elevations) through the \nweekend. The air mass will gradually warm, although even Sat/Sun\nhighs should only reach the mid 80s in valley locations, which \nis near normal for late July. There will also likely be patchy \nfog developing each night in favored sheltered valleys. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe pattern looks to become more unsettled early to middle of \nnext week, as an upper level trough becomes established across \nSE Canada, the Great Lakes into the Northeast. A lead short-wave\ntrough brings the next chance of showers/T-storms on Mon, \nfollowed by more widespread activity on Tue as a surface wave \npotentially tracks east across the area. Severe weather \npotential is uncertain at this time due to questionable \ninstability/shear and timing. PWATs forecast to remain within 1 \nSTDEV of normal, so any locally heavy rain will likely be \nmodulated by where convection occurs. The showers/T-storms could\nlinger into Wed if the upper trough moves slower, although \nlatest trends indicate a more progressive trough.",
